1
00:01:03,397 --> 00:01:11,397
<i>When peace, like a
river Attendeth my way</i>

2
00:01:14,841 --> 00:01:22,473
<i>When sorrows
Like sea billows roll</i>

3
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:27,420
<i>Whatever my lot</i>

4
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:36,088
<i>Thou hast taught
me to say It is well</i>

5
00:01:36,396 --> 00:01:43,326
<i>It is well with my soul</i>

6
00:01:44,004 --> 00:01:47,964
<i>It is well</i>

7
00:01:49,176 --> 00:01:57,176
<i>With my soul It is well</i>

8
00:01:57,851 --> 00:02:04,951
<i>It is well with my soul</i>

9
00:02:10,197 --> 00:02:18,197
<i>Oh, Lord, haste the day
When my faith shall be sight</i>

10
00:02:19,906 --> 00:02:27,245
<i>The clouds be
rolled back As a scroll</i>

11
00:02:27,414 --> 00:02:35,414
<i>The trump shall resound
And the Lord shall descend</i>

12
00:02:36,857 --> 00:02:44,857
<i>Even so, it is well with my soul</i>

13
00:02:46,299 --> 00:02:50,862
<i>It is well</i>

14
00:02:51,371 --> 00:02:59,371
<i>With my soul It
is well, it is well</i>

15
00:03:03,884 --> 00:03:11,884
<i>With my soul With my soul</i> Whew.

16
00:03:21,768 --> 00:03:24,397
Good. Not bad. Not bad.

17
00:03:24,571 --> 00:03:26,767
And Jerri's pretty
good. What say?

18
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:30,101
- Yeah.
- Jerri.

19
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:34,775
Okay, let's stop and
I'll see you on Tuesday.

20
00:03:34,948 --> 00:03:36,974
- Okay.
- Tuesday.

21
00:03:39,319 --> 00:03:41,379
- That was wonderful.
- Oh, thanks.

22
00:03:41,555 --> 00:03:43,235
This choir really means
a great deal to me.

23
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:45,325
- You've added a lot to it.
- Well, thank you.

24
00:03:45,492 --> 00:03:47,222
Ben didn't say what
a fine singer you are.

25
00:03:47,394 --> 00:03:49,886
Ha, ha. Thanks.

26
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:58,666
- You're staring.
- I am?

27
00:03:58,839 --> 00:04:01,308
You haven't taken your eyes
off Claire the whole rehearsal.

28
00:04:01,475 --> 00:04:04,445
It's not nice to
stare at a deacon.

29
00:04:04,811 --> 00:04:06,473
I'm so glad you
talked me into this.

30
00:04:06,646 --> 00:04:07,773
I like singing in the choir.

31
00:04:07,948 --> 00:04:10,179
Although singing a solo
scares me half to death.

32
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:13,149
Well, being a little afraid
makes you sing better.

33
00:04:14,521 --> 00:04:15,921
- I suppose so.
- Yeah.

34
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:18,115
What's his name again?

35
00:04:18,291 --> 00:04:20,192
- Oh, Jimmy Nash.
- Yeah.

36
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:21,988
Is he married?

37
00:04:22,162 --> 00:04:25,394
- As far as I know, he lives on his own.
- Yeah.

38
00:04:25,565 --> 00:04:27,659
Seems like a nice guy.

39
00:04:27,834 --> 00:04:29,132
He's a guy, all right.

40
00:04:29,302 --> 00:04:31,362
I'm not sure about the
nice part all the time.

41
00:04:33,974 --> 00:04:36,894
Where are your eyes, ref? Come on,
he just got the hell fouled out of him.

42
00:04:37,043 --> 00:04:39,171
- Come on, ref.
- What's your problem, ref?

43
00:04:39,346 --> 00:04:41,247
He's all over me.
Get in the game, ref.

44
00:04:41,414 --> 00:04:43,542
Come on, ref! Come on!

45
00:04:43,717 --> 00:04:46,243
Forget the bozo, Sipes.
Come on, run the plays.

46
00:04:46,419 --> 00:04:48,700
You would've run the plays,
you wouldn't have been fouled.

47
00:04:49,222 --> 00:04:51,248
Are you sure that's
the same Jimmy Nash?

48
00:04:51,424 --> 00:04:55,122
No, now it's Jimmy Nash, coach.
Different animal. Hey, Jimmy!

49
00:04:55,295 --> 00:04:56,991
Will you call the foul?

50
00:04:57,164 --> 00:04:59,156
Is that a whistle in your
mouth or your thumb?

51
00:04:59,332 --> 00:05:01,392
"Animal" being the key word.

52
00:05:02,569 --> 00:05:05,971
Pass the ball. Pass the ball.

53
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:07,735
Defense, move it!

54
00:05:09,342 --> 00:05:10,833
Come on, guys!

55
00:05:12,279 --> 00:05:14,305
- What are you looking at?
- Claire.

56
00:05:14,481 --> 00:05:16,814
All right! Yeah!

57
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:18,843
- She's in the choir.
- Yeah.

58
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:20,043
Alto.

59
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:22,849
Yeah. I've always
had a thing for altos.

60
00:05:26,092 --> 00:05:28,721
- Good job!
- Hustle the ball!

61
00:05:33,733 --> 00:05:35,031
Hey, hey, all right!

62
00:05:36,703 --> 00:05:38,171
She looks nice, doesn't she?

63
00:05:41,208 --> 00:05:42,938
Groans nice too.

64
00:05:44,211 --> 00:05:46,237
- Defense.
- Come on.

65
00:05:51,718 --> 00:05:54,381
Sixty-seven to 64, two minutes.

66
00:05:54,554 --> 00:05:56,113
What do you think?

67
00:05:56,523 --> 00:05:57,855
I think she's a beauty.

68
00:06:02,963 --> 00:06:05,091
Pass it!

69
00:06:05,932 --> 00:06:08,959
Set it up. Set it up. Barry.

70
00:06:09,502 --> 00:06:11,630
Sipes. The play, Sipes.

71
00:06:12,539 --> 00:06:14,531
Just pass the ball!

72
00:06:18,945 --> 00:06:20,072
No, the play!

73
00:06:20,247 --> 00:06:22,409
Referee, come on, damn it.

74
00:06:22,816 --> 00:06:24,256
What the hell's the
matter with you?

75
00:06:24,417 --> 00:06:26,977
That ape's been checking him
all night and you haven't called it.

76
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:29,885
- Sit down, coach.
- You see this? This is a foul.

77
00:06:30,056 --> 00:06:32,416
Call it. You put this whistle
in your mouth and you blow it.

78
00:06:32,559 --> 00:06:33,583
I'm warning you, coach.

79
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:36,000
What, you're too dumb, too
blind? Why aren't you calling?

80
00:06:36,162 --> 00:06:38,654
Are you in somebody's pocket?
That why you haven't made a call?

81
00:06:38,832 --> 00:06:41,666
- You're out of here.
- Oh, finally, I get a word from you!

82
00:07:28,848 --> 00:07:31,215
You know what you
ought to do, coach?

83
00:07:32,652 --> 00:07:36,089
You ought to sign up for one of
those anger-management courses.

84
00:07:36,256 --> 00:07:39,021
I sold a car to a guy who
gives them last month.

85
00:07:39,192 --> 00:07:40,472
I could probably get you a deal.

86
00:07:41,261 --> 00:07:43,856
Just go outside and watch
the rest of the game, okay, Red?

87
00:07:44,030 --> 00:07:46,329
No, it's too painful.
We're gonna lose.

88
00:07:46,499 --> 00:07:49,833
That was plain as day long before
that little temper tantrum of yours.

89
00:07:50,003 --> 00:07:52,472
That's because that ref
made it a five-on-six game.

90
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:54,969
No.

91
00:07:55,141 --> 00:07:58,111
We need some new
blood, and that's a fact.

92
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:01,880
I understand there's a kid in Maryland
that can just shoot the lights out.

93
00:08:02,048 --> 00:08:04,108
Daniel Downing, I
think his name is.

94
00:08:04,284 --> 00:08:06,844
Every school this side of the
Mississippi is looking at that kid.

95
00:08:06,987 --> 00:08:08,512
We don't have a shot at him.

96
00:08:09,823 --> 00:08:12,452
Well, now, what kind of
self-defeating attitude is that?

97
00:08:12,625 --> 00:08:15,789
We got as good a shot
at it as anybody else.

98
00:08:15,962 --> 00:08:18,761
It's just a matter of offering
him the right incentives, that's all.

99
00:08:18,932 --> 00:08:22,300
I've never paid for a player in my
life, and I'm not about to start now.

100
00:08:22,469 --> 00:08:25,166
Money? No.

101
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:27,705
Working with you,
Jimmy, that's the incentive.

102
00:08:27,874 --> 00:08:30,241
The kid starts his freshman
year, he's a star right off.

103
00:08:30,410 --> 00:08:33,107
He gets the immediate
attention of all the pro scouts,

104
00:08:33,279 --> 00:08:36,681
not to mention a fine
college education, of course.

105
00:08:36,850 --> 00:08:39,410
That's the incentive
I'm talking about.

106
00:08:39,586 --> 00:08:41,782
Recruiting is my job.

107
00:08:41,955 --> 00:08:45,687
Yeah, but you see, as president
of the Baxter Booster Club,

108
00:08:45,859 --> 00:08:48,590
I worry about that too, Jimbo.

109
00:08:48,962 --> 00:08:50,362
I mean, we gotta work together.

110
00:08:51,131 --> 00:08:55,091
You get Daniel Downing down here
and just be your sweet, charming self.

111
00:08:55,268 --> 00:08:57,032
That's all I ask.

112
00:08:58,938 --> 00:09:04,605
Just because this year is a bust,
doesn't mean next year has to be.

113
00:09:13,953 --> 00:09:17,117
- Oh, man.
- Man.

114
00:09:19,092 --> 00:09:22,028
You wanna play on my
team, you stick with the plays.

115
00:09:22,195 --> 00:09:23,458
Shot in the fourth quarter?

116
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:25,826
The play was busted.

117
00:09:26,266 --> 00:09:27,962
Not from where I was sitting.

118
00:09:28,134 --> 00:09:30,334
Well, maybe you ought to
try it out on the court, coach.

119
00:09:30,470 --> 00:09:33,565
Don't you ever talk to me
like that again. You got that?

120
00:09:35,008 --> 00:09:36,476
Just relax.

121
00:09:51,891 --> 00:09:53,757
All right, come on,
kid, use your body.

122
00:09:53,927 --> 00:09:56,487
You're playing
college kids next year.

123
00:10:21,921 --> 00:10:23,561
All right, give me
something from outside.

124
00:10:23,690 --> 00:10:25,090
Kid's pretty good.

125
00:10:25,258 --> 00:10:28,524
Well, that or the old
man is starting to lose it.

126
00:10:28,695 --> 00:10:30,288
- Hard to tell which.
- Ha, ha.

127
00:10:30,463 --> 00:10:33,160
- Hi, Ron.
- Mr. Tolman.

128
00:10:39,339 --> 00:10:42,173
- All right, that's enough.
- I was hoping you'd say that.

129
00:10:42,342 --> 00:10:43,935
- I'm impressed.
- So am I.

130
00:10:44,110 --> 00:10:45,976
I told you he was
something, didn't I, coach?

131
00:10:46,613 --> 00:10:48,275
Let's go talk in my office.

132
00:10:48,448 --> 00:10:50,314
Good idea.

133
00:10:54,754 --> 00:10:56,780
Well, I've looked
at a lot of programs,

134
00:10:56,956 --> 00:10:59,721
and a lot of programs
have looked at me.

135
00:10:59,893 --> 00:11:02,863
Well, Danny, this is one program
that would love to have you.

136
00:11:03,029 --> 00:11:04,509
If you're looking
for a good education

137
00:11:04,664 --> 00:11:06,758
and a chance to play
some quality basketball,

138
00:11:07,267 --> 00:11:08,826
this is the place to be.

139
00:11:09,102 --> 00:11:10,866
It's starting to look
like that to me too.

140
00:11:11,571 --> 00:11:13,039
You think you might
wanna go with us?

141
00:11:13,306 --> 00:11:15,832
- I know I do.
- Whoa, coach.

142
00:11:16,009 --> 00:11:18,945
I mean, that boy is not
only talented, he's smart.

143
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:23,343
So when do we discuss the
details of our arrangement?

144
00:11:23,516 --> 00:11:26,213
Danny, why don't you
go on back to the motel...?

145
00:11:26,386 --> 00:11:28,014
What arrangement?

146
00:11:29,522 --> 00:11:32,856
The 30 grand in
cash and the new car.

147
00:11:33,026 --> 00:11:34,221
A sports car, right?

148
00:11:39,132 --> 00:11:40,395
You promised him that?

149
00:11:42,001 --> 00:11:45,870
Jimmy, there's certain things
that you just gotta be realistic about.

150
00:11:46,272 --> 00:11:48,764
Listen, son, no matter
what he's promised you,

151
00:11:48,942 --> 00:11:50,410
we don't pay for players here.

152
00:11:50,577 --> 00:11:52,705
If that's what you're after,
you can get out of here.

153
00:11:52,879 --> 00:11:56,407
- But I...
- Look, look, this is not a payoff.

154
00:11:56,583 --> 00:11:59,553
This is an investment
in this university's future.

155
00:11:59,719 --> 00:12:02,621
And you too, get out!

156
00:12:03,189 --> 00:12:05,317
All right, Jimmy. All
right, all right, take it easy.

157
00:12:05,491 --> 00:12:07,960
Don't tell me to take it
easy! Get out of here!

158
00:12:08,595 --> 00:12:10,496
They're back, man.
Here he comes.

159
00:12:10,663 --> 00:12:13,633
This is my program and I'll run
it the way I want! You got that?

160
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:16,668
I don't wanna see either
one of you around here again!

161
00:12:18,171 --> 00:12:19,662
What happened?

162
00:13:39,052 --> 00:13:40,452
Yup.

163
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:45,558
"Death occurred between
9 and 11," it says here.

164
00:13:45,725 --> 00:13:48,661
I was home all night by myself.

165
00:13:49,362 --> 00:13:50,990
Did anybody see
you or talk to you?

166
00:13:51,164 --> 00:13:53,463
I just told you, I
was by myself.

167
00:13:53,633 --> 00:13:57,832
Jimmy, why are you
so calm and nice at choir

168
00:13:58,004 --> 00:14:02,442
and so hostile other
places, especially basketball?

169
00:14:02,608 --> 00:14:04,941
Because nobody
messes with me in choir.

170
00:14:05,111 --> 00:14:09,845
Well, we gotta get this stuff straight,
so you might as well just calm down.

171
00:14:11,851 --> 00:14:15,947
The police found one of
your whistles in his hand,

172
00:14:16,122 --> 00:14:18,091
and the police are saying

173
00:14:18,257 --> 00:14:21,284
that he ripped it off your
neck during a violent struggle.

174
00:14:21,861 --> 00:14:23,621
I've got whistles
hanging all over my office.

175
00:14:23,763 --> 00:14:25,959
Anybody could've stolen
it and planted it there.

176
00:14:26,132 --> 00:14:30,092
And you had words with
Red in the gym yesterday.

177
00:14:30,370 --> 00:14:32,703
Yes. He went behind my back.

178
00:14:32,872 --> 00:14:34,632
He told this kid that
I was trying to recruit

179
00:14:34,774 --> 00:14:36,134
that he was gonna
give him $30,000

180
00:14:36,275 --> 00:14:39,211
and a brand-new sports car if
he'd come and play at Baxter.

181
00:14:40,279 --> 00:14:41,611
I guess that made you mad.

182
00:14:41,781 --> 00:14:43,909
Yeah, it made me mad.
I could've killed him.

183
00:14:45,051 --> 00:14:47,043
Jimbo, I wouldn't
be saying that.

184
00:14:49,288 --> 00:14:51,018
I didn't kill him, Ben.

185
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:53,955
I swear to you,
I did not kill him.

186
00:14:54,127 --> 00:14:56,289
I'm being framed.

187
00:15:45,111 --> 00:15:47,444
Hi, Claire, Cliff.

188
00:15:48,714 --> 00:15:51,513
Claire, Cliff Lewis from choir.

189
00:15:52,618 --> 00:15:54,849
Claire, Cliff, what's happening?

190
00:15:55,021 --> 00:15:56,512
That's really bad.

191
00:15:56,689 --> 00:15:57,713
- Cliff.
- What?

192
00:15:57,890 --> 00:16:00,724
- What are you doing?
- Nothing.

193
00:16:00,893 --> 00:16:02,919
What's to eat around
here? I'm starved.

194
00:16:03,095 --> 00:16:06,759
Ah, he's got ham and
cheese. You want a sandwich?

195
00:16:07,533 --> 00:16:10,053
Well, if you must know, I was
just thinking about calling Claire

196
00:16:10,203 --> 00:16:12,434
and I was rehearsing
what I was gonna say.

197
00:16:12,605 --> 00:16:13,766
You're gonna ask her out?

198
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:16,671
Well, I thought about it, but I
don't know if it's such a good idea.

199
00:16:16,843 --> 00:16:18,106
Why, you nervous?

200
00:16:18,277 --> 00:16:21,247
Yeah. But that's
not the problem.

201
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:25,919
You're afraid she'll say no?

202
00:16:26,085 --> 00:16:29,453
Yeah, probably, but
that's not the problem.

203
00:16:29,622 --> 00:16:31,147
Cliff, what's the problem?

204
00:16:31,324 --> 00:16:34,226
Well, I don't know if she's married.
I don't know anything about her.

205
00:16:34,393 --> 00:16:36,021
Well, why don't you ask her?

206
00:16:36,195 --> 00:16:40,530
Me? Oh, no, no, no, I couldn't
do that. I couldn't do that. No.

207
00:16:42,468 --> 00:16:45,267
- You want me to?
- No. No.

208
00:16:45,905 --> 00:16:47,737
Yes. Yeah.

209
00:16:47,907 --> 00:16:50,399
Yeah, all I wanna do, I wanna
maybe take her for a drive

210
00:16:50,576 --> 00:16:53,671
or to a movie or out to dinner.

211
00:16:53,846 --> 00:16:55,246
Out to lunch. Heh.

212
00:16:58,251 --> 00:17:00,516
Let me ask you a question.

213
00:17:02,355 --> 00:17:04,824
Why don't you have
a man in your life?

214
00:17:04,991 --> 00:17:07,756
I told you, I just broke
up with somebody.

215
00:17:07,927 --> 00:17:10,453
I figured you just told me
that so I wouldn't ask you out.

216
00:17:11,631 --> 00:17:12,792
You didn't believe me?

217
00:17:12,965 --> 00:17:14,593
No.

218
00:17:16,836 --> 00:17:18,828
- Well, you were right.
- Ah.

219
00:17:19,005 --> 00:17:21,099
The truth is

220
00:17:21,974 --> 00:17:23,636
I'm staying not
interested in anyone

221
00:17:23,809 --> 00:17:27,177
- because I've got really bad taste.
- Hmm.

222
00:17:27,346 --> 00:17:29,577
I've spent most of my
life attracted to guys

223
00:17:29,749 --> 00:17:33,015
with great faces,
incredible bodies,

224
00:17:33,185 --> 00:17:34,905
but six months later,
I wake up and I think:

225
00:17:35,054 --> 00:17:36,352
"What am I doing?"

226
00:17:36,522 --> 00:17:39,458
I just can't seem to
pick the right kind of guy.

227
00:17:42,228 --> 00:17:45,721
So I've decided that I'm not
gonna get involved with anybody.

228
00:17:46,165 --> 00:17:49,624
Unless I know the man really well.
He's gonna have to be my best friend.

229
00:17:49,802 --> 00:17:50,997
Yup.

230
00:17:51,170 --> 00:17:53,833
And then we'll see
what happens after that.

231
00:17:54,006 --> 00:17:57,238
Mm, mm. You're right. You're
right, you're right, you're right.

232
00:17:57,410 --> 00:17:59,208
I have to get to know Claire.

233
00:17:59,378 --> 00:18:01,176
That's what I'd do.

234
00:18:01,347 --> 00:18:03,077
But you're gonna
check into her first.

235
00:18:03,249 --> 00:18:04,808
Sure. No problem.

236
00:18:04,984 --> 00:18:06,344
- All right.
- You can count on me.

237
00:18:06,485 --> 00:18:09,182
Great, great,
great. That's great.

238
00:18:09,355 --> 00:18:12,484
I'll do something
nice for you sometime.

239
00:18:17,496 --> 00:18:22,833
<i>Go not far from me</i>

240
00:18:23,102 --> 00:18:31,102
<i>Far from me, O God
Cast me not away</i>

241
00:18:34,246 --> 00:18:42,246
<i>Cast me not away
In the time of age</i>

242
00:18:46,959 --> 00:18:54,959
<i>Cast me not away
In the time of age</i>

243
00:18:59,138 --> 00:19:01,664
<i>Forsake me not</i>

244
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:08,846
<i>Forsake me not Forsake me not</i>

245
00:19:09,015 --> 00:19:17,015
<i>When my strength Fail Me</i>

246
00:19:25,798 --> 00:19:27,596
Wonderful, wonderful.

247
00:19:27,767 --> 00:19:30,207
If we get any better, we're
gonna have to get a bigger church.

248
00:19:31,537 --> 00:19:32,766
Let's go ahead and take five.

249
00:19:32,938 --> 00:19:35,237
- Okay.
- Five minutes.

250
00:19:37,343 --> 00:19:39,175
Excuse me.

251
00:19:40,579 --> 00:19:41,638
She smiled at me.

252
00:19:41,814 --> 00:19:43,774
Guess you haven't had a
chance to talk to her yet.

253
00:19:43,916 --> 00:19:47,375
She's recently divorced, no kids,
she works for the gas company,

254
00:19:47,553 --> 00:19:49,385
does a lot of volunteer
work for the church.

255
00:19:49,555 --> 00:19:52,787
She likes to bird-watch
and water-ski.

256
00:19:52,958 --> 00:19:55,689
It's too bad you haven't had
a chance to find anything out.

257
00:19:55,861 --> 00:19:57,022
And she likes to run.

258
00:19:57,196 --> 00:19:59,461
No, run? You're
kidding. Oh, that's great.

259
00:19:59,632 --> 00:20:01,931
Oh, can you find
out when and where?

260
00:20:02,101 --> 00:20:05,367
Parkwood High School, 5:30 p.m.,
Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays.

261
00:20:05,538 --> 00:20:09,066
Honestly, Cliff, I am so far ahead
of you, you can't even see me.

262
00:20:10,876 --> 00:20:13,539
- See you next week.
- Okay, see you.

263
00:20:16,449 --> 00:20:18,714
Well, the board of trustees
called me this morning.

264
00:20:18,884 --> 00:20:21,183
Said I can't coach
until after the trial.

265
00:20:21,353 --> 00:20:24,152
Unless and until I'm
acquitted. That's how they put it.

266
00:20:24,323 --> 00:20:26,383
The board, huh? Hmm.

267
00:20:27,326 --> 00:20:29,659
Oh, great, a cop.

268
00:20:29,895 --> 00:20:31,124
Now, what does he want?

269
00:20:31,297 --> 00:20:33,994
- Hmm.
- Hello again, coach.

270
00:20:34,166 --> 00:20:38,001
So it's true, you really
do sing in the choir.

271
00:20:38,270 --> 00:20:39,704
What do you want?

272
00:20:39,872 --> 00:20:42,535
Red Tolman's house
was burglarized last night.

273
00:20:42,708 --> 00:20:46,008
Wife heard noises.
Called 911 around 10:15.

274
00:20:46,412 --> 00:20:47,505
So what?

275
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:49,273
Coach Nash was with me

276
00:20:49,448 --> 00:20:53,510
from, oh, 7:30 till about
11 working on his defense.

277
00:20:53,686 --> 00:20:56,178
He didn't burglarize the house.

278
00:20:56,655 --> 00:20:59,625
- Well, that takes care of that.
- Mm-hm.

279
00:20:59,792 --> 00:21:01,385
You sing in the choir too, Ben?

280
00:21:01,560 --> 00:21:03,426
Oh, yeah, every Sunday.

281
00:21:03,596 --> 00:21:05,531
You ought to come sometime.

282
00:21:05,698 --> 00:21:06,859
I'll keep it in mind.

283
00:21:07,032 --> 00:21:11,436
Oh, yeah, going to church is
a good habit to start and keep.

284
00:21:11,604 --> 00:21:13,334
Makes you feel good.

285
00:21:13,506 --> 00:21:16,237
Calms jittery nerves.

286
00:21:16,976 --> 00:21:18,911
Look at Jimmy.

287
00:21:27,186 --> 00:21:29,246
Joe, if you could find
that out, I'd appreciate it.

288
00:21:29,421 --> 00:21:31,686
- Yeah.
- Thanks.

289
00:21:34,326 --> 00:21:37,262
Red and I met at Baxter.

290
00:21:38,063 --> 00:21:42,831
At a pep rally for a basketball
game against Leland University.

291
00:21:45,738 --> 00:21:49,698
Did he keep his business
files here or at the dealership?

292
00:21:50,109 --> 00:21:52,135
At the dealership.

293
00:21:52,311 --> 00:21:55,804
So most of the stuff that
was stolen out of this cabinet

294
00:21:55,981 --> 00:21:58,007
was probably of
a personal nature?

295
00:21:59,985 --> 00:22:02,784
I suppose so, yes.

296
00:22:13,666 --> 00:22:16,966
Do you have any idea
what these numbers are?

297
00:22:21,507 --> 00:22:22,531
No.

298
00:22:24,076 --> 00:22:25,669
I'll take it in the other room.

299
00:22:25,845 --> 00:22:27,370
Okay.

300
00:22:39,925 --> 00:22:43,327
- Hey, Floyd.
- Yeah. Miss Stone, what's up?

301
00:22:43,495 --> 00:22:44,929
- You done working in here?
- Yep.

302
00:22:45,097 --> 00:22:47,430
In about five minutes, we'll
be done working out here.

303
00:22:47,833 --> 00:22:48,857
What you got?

304
00:22:49,235 --> 00:22:52,603
A nice clear
podiatric calling card.

305
00:22:52,771 --> 00:22:54,205
A footprint, huh?

306
00:22:54,373 --> 00:22:57,138
A big footprint.

307
00:23:00,246 --> 00:23:01,305
Cool.

308
00:23:06,352 --> 00:23:09,151
Keep moving. Get the open shot.

309
00:23:12,558 --> 00:23:14,618
That's it. That's it.

310
00:23:16,695 --> 00:23:18,220
All right.

311
00:23:18,397 --> 00:23:19,865
No, Barry.

312
00:23:20,032 --> 00:23:21,796
You made your cut too soon.

313
00:23:21,967 --> 00:23:23,799
Let the play develop.

314
00:23:23,969 --> 00:23:25,801
You're not supposed
to be coaching, Jimmy.

315
00:23:25,971 --> 00:23:27,963
I mean, if this kid
would just concentrate.

316
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:31,167
Hmm. I'll tell you what,
let's talk in your office.

317
00:23:31,343 --> 00:23:35,178
- What are you doing?
- Come on, come on. Come on. Ah.

318
00:23:35,347 --> 00:23:37,509
Pike, that is your shot.
Take it when it's there.

319
00:23:37,683 --> 00:23:39,652
Come on. Come on.

320
00:23:39,818 --> 00:23:41,684
Send it back out.

321
00:23:42,888 --> 00:23:44,982
- Oh.
- Go on to your office.

322
00:23:45,157 --> 00:23:48,559
I'll meet you there.
Go on. Go on.

323
00:23:51,664 --> 00:23:56,329
Now, I hear the police found
a footprint made by your shoe

324
00:23:56,502 --> 00:23:58,971
- outside Red's house.
- Yeah.

325
00:23:59,138 --> 00:24:02,336
They think you broke
in there last night.

326
00:24:02,508 --> 00:24:05,603
I used to go to parties
there all the time,

327
00:24:05,778 --> 00:24:08,441
so even if that was my
footprint, I didn't break in.

328
00:24:08,614 --> 00:24:11,049
You always enter the house
through the window, Sipes?

329
00:24:11,216 --> 00:24:13,515
You broke in because there
was proof Red was paying you.

330
00:24:13,686 --> 00:24:14,984
That's a lie. That's a lie.

331
00:24:15,154 --> 00:24:16,645
- Jimmy...
- How much did you make?

332
00:24:16,822 --> 00:24:20,691
Hey, at least I didn't kill anybody!
You'll never coach again! You'll never...

333
00:24:20,859 --> 00:24:22,885
And you'll never play again!

334
00:24:23,062 --> 00:24:26,191
I want you, coach. Come on,
let's go. Right now. Right now.

335
00:24:26,365 --> 00:24:28,197
- I top you, my friend.
- Right now!

336
00:24:28,367 --> 00:24:30,734
- Let's get out of here.
- Right now. Come on.

337
00:24:39,979 --> 00:24:43,006
Claire. Hey. Cliff,
from the choir.

338
00:24:43,182 --> 00:24:46,016
- Yeah. Sure. How you doing?
- Oh, great, great. How you doing?

339
00:24:46,185 --> 00:24:48,654
- Good.
- Good. That's great.

340
00:24:48,821 --> 00:24:50,301
- You come running here a lot?
- Yeah.

341
00:24:50,456 --> 00:24:51,480
- Yeah?
- All the time.

342
00:24:51,657 --> 00:24:54,593
- Really?
- Same 16 laps, year in, year out. Heh.

343
00:24:54,760 --> 00:24:58,697
Oh. Well, why don't, uh...? Why
don't we go running sometime?

344
00:24:58,864 --> 00:25:00,355
You know, I could
show you some routes

345
00:25:00,532 --> 00:25:02,194
and broaden your
horizons a little.

346
00:25:02,368 --> 00:25:05,065
- Yeah. That'd be great.
- Great.

347
00:25:05,237 --> 00:25:08,401
Well, that's it for me. I'll
see you at choir practice?

348
00:25:08,574 --> 00:25:10,543
- Yeah, great. Great.
- Okay.

349
00:25:10,709 --> 00:25:13,508
Nice running into you. Bye-bye.

350
00:25:13,679 --> 00:25:14,942
See you next time.

351
00:25:16,215 --> 00:25:18,377
I used to think that
losing your temper like that

352
00:25:18,550 --> 00:25:19,990
was something
you did just for show,

353
00:25:20,152 --> 00:25:21,882
but you got a real
problem, you know that?

354
00:25:22,054 --> 00:25:23,113
Yeah, yeah, I know.

355
00:25:23,288 --> 00:25:25,368
You lose it like that in
front of a judge and jury...

356
00:25:25,457 --> 00:25:27,358
Okay, okay, I get the picture.

357
00:25:27,526 --> 00:25:29,688
You'd better get the picture.

358
00:25:32,631 --> 00:25:36,625
So you think this
boy, Ron Sipes...?

359
00:25:36,802 --> 00:25:40,261
You think Red was paying him
on the sly to come here and play?

360
00:25:40,906 --> 00:25:43,876
God knows how many other
kids he tried to recruit like that.

361
00:25:44,443 --> 00:25:47,811
I'm sure God knows a lot
more than we think he does.

362
00:25:47,980 --> 00:25:52,350
Well, we'll get some subpoenas
and have a look at a few bank records,

363
00:25:52,518 --> 00:25:53,713
see what we come out with.

364
00:25:53,886 --> 00:25:57,379
Oh, this was found
on Red's desk.

365
00:25:57,556 --> 00:25:59,388
Mean anything to you?

366
00:26:00,726 --> 00:26:01,750
No.

367
00:26:03,929 --> 00:26:07,559
Oh. Hi, Barry. Come on in.

368
00:26:07,733 --> 00:26:09,929
Ben, this is Barry Glover.

369
00:26:10,135 --> 00:26:11,330
- Oh, Ben Matlock.
- Hi.

370
00:26:11,503 --> 00:26:13,802
That slam dunk of yours
just takes my breath away.

371
00:26:13,972 --> 00:26:15,964
Ha, ha. Thanks. Ahem.

372
00:26:16,141 --> 00:26:17,666
Uh, I need to talk to you.

373
00:26:17,843 --> 00:26:19,436
About?

374
00:26:19,611 --> 00:26:22,410
The, uh, Red Tolman thing.

375
00:26:22,581 --> 00:26:26,074
Well, Mr. Matlock is my attorney,
son. You can talk in front of him.

376
00:26:26,251 --> 00:26:28,049
The police came by
my place this morning

377
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:30,314
and asked me a
bunch of questions.

378
00:26:30,489 --> 00:26:32,219
What did they want with you?

379
00:26:32,391 --> 00:26:36,351
Well, see, the night of the
murder, I went over to your house.

380
00:26:37,396 --> 00:26:41,094
You told me if I ever had trouble in
any of my classes to make sure I got help,

381
00:26:41,266 --> 00:26:43,792
and Ancient World History has
been giving me fits this semester.

382
00:26:44,703 --> 00:26:47,639
Anyway, uh, I rang
the bell at the front door,

383
00:26:48,307 --> 00:26:51,277
went around to the side,
but you weren't there.

384
00:26:51,443 --> 00:26:54,208
I guess one of your neighbors
saw me and recognized me.

385
00:26:54,980 --> 00:26:56,004
What time was that?

386
00:26:56,448 --> 00:26:59,612
Around 9:30.

387
00:27:00,152 --> 00:27:03,645
I couldn't lie. I mean, your
car was gone and everything.

388
00:27:03,822 --> 00:27:05,848
Don't worry about it.

389
00:27:06,158 --> 00:27:07,387
I'm sorry, coach.

390
00:27:07,559 --> 00:27:09,255
It's okay.

391
00:27:09,428 --> 00:27:11,363
You did the right thing.

392
00:27:11,763 --> 00:27:13,891
You just work on that jumper.

393
00:27:15,701 --> 00:27:17,533
Where were you?

394
00:27:19,538 --> 00:27:20,836
I can't tell you.

395
00:27:21,206 --> 00:27:23,198
You lied to me and
you lied to the police.

396
00:27:23,375 --> 00:27:25,674
- Ben...
- I'm working my butt off for you.

397
00:27:25,844 --> 00:27:28,973
Look, temper, Ben. People
lie to their lawyers all the time.

398
00:27:29,148 --> 00:27:31,515
Not to this lawyer, they
don't. I want the truth.

399
00:27:31,683 --> 00:27:33,083
I didn't kill Red Tolman.

400
00:27:34,887 --> 00:27:37,982
I'm sorry about the lie,
but I didn't kill the man.

401
00:27:50,802 --> 00:27:55,137
I understand you play basketball for
the Baxter Cougars. Is that correct?

402
00:27:55,307 --> 00:27:56,468
I used to.

403
00:27:56,642 --> 00:27:59,544
I've been declared
ineligible by the NCAA.

404
00:27:59,711 --> 00:28:01,703
And why's that?

405
00:28:03,882 --> 00:28:05,680
I took money from Red Tolman.

406
00:28:06,351 --> 00:28:09,685
The decedent was paying you
to play basketball for Baxter?

407
00:28:10,622 --> 00:28:11,817
Yeah.

408
00:28:11,990 --> 00:28:15,791
See, he gave me a
job at his car dealership,

409
00:28:15,961 --> 00:28:17,862
only I didn't have to show up.

410
00:28:18,030 --> 00:28:19,521
I just collected a paycheck.

411
00:28:20,165 --> 00:28:24,000
Did anyone else know
about this arrangement?

412
00:28:24,169 --> 00:28:25,637
Yes, ma'am.

413
00:28:25,804 --> 00:28:28,899
The man who turned
me in, Coach Nash.

414
00:28:29,074 --> 00:28:30,804
- That's a lie. That's a lie.
- Order.

415
00:28:30,976 --> 00:28:32,736
That's a damn lie, and
he damn well knows it!

416
00:28:32,878 --> 00:28:34,073
- Time out.
- Order.

417
00:28:34,246 --> 00:28:35,509
- He's lying!
- Order.

418
00:28:35,681 --> 00:28:36,842
- Order.
- Time out.

419
00:28:37,015 --> 00:28:38,745
Order.

420
00:28:38,917 --> 00:28:40,351
Mr. Matlock.

421
00:28:40,652 --> 00:28:41,847
Five minutes.

422
00:28:44,156 --> 00:28:45,488
You wanna get mad?

423
00:28:45,657 --> 00:28:46,955
Here, get mad.

424
00:28:47,125 --> 00:28:49,085
Jump up and down on it.
Throw it against the wall.

425
00:28:49,228 --> 00:28:50,252
Throw it out the window.

426
00:28:50,429 --> 00:28:53,524
Do any asinine thing you want
to to get it out of your system.

427
00:28:53,699 --> 00:28:55,759
- Ben, look...
- You know how I told the lieutenant

428
00:28:55,934 --> 00:28:59,098
that going to church calms
jittery nerves? Not you.

429
00:28:59,571 --> 00:29:01,062
The old hymn we like to sing:

430
00:29:01,240 --> 00:29:04,404
"In that far off sweet forever,
just beyond the shining river,

431
00:29:04,576 --> 00:29:06,875
when they ring the golden
bells for you and me."

432
00:29:07,045 --> 00:29:09,245
You keep yelling and cussing
in the courtroom like that,

433
00:29:09,414 --> 00:29:11,906
they ain't gonna ring
no golden bells for you.

434
00:29:17,089 --> 00:29:20,457
You just testified that
Coach Nash knew all along

435
00:29:20,626 --> 00:29:23,152
that Red Tolman was
paying you under the table,

436
00:29:23,328 --> 00:29:27,129
and that Coach Nash turned
you in to the NCAA. Is that right?

437
00:29:27,299 --> 00:29:30,167
- Yes, it is.
- Then I just have one other question.

438
00:29:30,335 --> 00:29:33,100
If Coach Nash knew all
along about those payments,

439
00:29:33,272 --> 00:29:36,572
why would he turn you in knowing that
you'd just turn around and turn him in?

440
00:29:36,742 --> 00:29:37,766
Objection.

441
00:29:38,210 --> 00:29:39,610
Calls for speculation.

442
00:29:40,078 --> 00:29:41,979
Yeah, I guess it
does. Nothing further.

443
00:29:44,616 --> 00:29:52,616
<i>The morning sun Dispels
the darkened cloud</i>

444
00:29:53,825 --> 00:30:01,825
<i>Sun of my soul, I cry aloud</i>

445
00:30:03,035 --> 00:30:11,035
<i>To thee for faith and
strength To carry on</i>

446
00:30:13,111 --> 00:30:21,111
<i>Through darkest
night Till break of dawn</i>

447
00:30:23,155 --> 00:30:31,155
<i>The morning sun
Gives birth to every day</i>

448
00:30:33,598 --> 00:30:41,598
<i>To thee, my God I kneel and pray</i>

449
00:30:48,046 --> 00:30:49,810
Better and better.

450
00:30:49,981 --> 00:30:51,701
We keep singing like
this, we get to heaven,

451
00:30:51,850 --> 00:30:53,284
we're gonna have a job.

452
00:30:55,053 --> 00:30:57,352
- Okay. See you next time.
- Okay.

453
00:30:57,522 --> 00:30:58,820
- Bye.
- Bye.

454
00:31:03,695 --> 00:31:04,890
Claire.

455
00:31:05,063 --> 00:31:06,793
- Hi. Ready to go for that run?
- Hey. Hi.

456
00:31:06,965 --> 00:31:08,934
- Um, yeah. Sure.
- Great.

457
00:31:09,101 --> 00:31:11,366
Why don't you meet me at
my house so I can change,

458
00:31:11,536 --> 00:31:13,402
- and we'll go from there?
- Great. Excellent.

459
00:31:13,572 --> 00:31:15,302
- Okay. Do you know where it is?
- Yeah.

460
00:31:15,474 --> 00:31:17,739
- Okay. Bye.
- Sounds good. All right.

461
00:32:17,202 --> 00:32:19,171
That was a good run, Cliff.

462
00:32:19,337 --> 00:32:21,966
I didn't even know
the park was there.

463
00:32:22,507 --> 00:32:25,773
Yeah, I discovered it when I
was training for the triathlon.

464
00:32:25,944 --> 00:32:27,606
You were in a triathlon?

465
00:32:27,779 --> 00:32:29,975
Yeah, a small one. It
was up in Wilmington.

466
00:32:30,148 --> 00:32:31,828
I didn't win or
anything, but I did finish.

467
00:32:31,983 --> 00:32:34,612
Well, that's great. That's
better than I could've done.

468
00:32:35,187 --> 00:32:37,122
I don't know. You seem
like you're in good shape.

469
00:32:37,289 --> 00:32:38,689
Thanks.

470
00:32:41,159 --> 00:32:45,290
Uh, well, I would invite you in
for some water or something,

471
00:32:45,464 --> 00:32:47,626
but I'm kind of
pressed for time.

472
00:32:47,799 --> 00:32:51,998
- Oh. We'll try it some other time, then.
- Definitely. Definitely.

473
00:32:52,170 --> 00:32:55,265
So, uh, I'll see you
at choir practice?

474
00:32:55,941 --> 00:32:57,409
Choir practice, right.

475
00:32:57,576 --> 00:33:00,205
- Okay. Bye.
- Okay. Bye.

476
00:33:30,342 --> 00:33:32,675
Mm. I'm all sweaty.

477
00:33:32,844 --> 00:33:34,437
- Running, huh?
- Mm-hm.

478
00:33:34,613 --> 00:33:35,945
With Cliff

479
00:33:36,114 --> 00:33:38,845
from the choir and
Ben Matlock's office.

480
00:33:39,017 --> 00:33:40,178
Oh.

481
00:33:40,352 --> 00:33:42,685
Well, he's been asking
and kind of flirting.

482
00:33:42,854 --> 00:33:44,584
I thought it wouldn't hurt.

483
00:33:44,756 --> 00:33:46,190
It's okay, isn't it?

484
00:33:46,358 --> 00:33:48,054
Sure.

485
00:33:48,660 --> 00:33:51,357
Mm. Don't you want
me to take a shower?

486
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:53,192
Later.

487
00:34:02,574 --> 00:34:04,839
- Hi, Claire.
- Jerri. What are you doing here?

488
00:34:05,911 --> 00:34:08,151
Ben wanted me to find out
why Jimmy lied about his alibi,

489
00:34:08,313 --> 00:34:10,179
so I've been following
him. Can I talk to him?

490
00:34:10,482 --> 00:34:12,280
- Let her in, Claire.
- Well...

491
00:34:19,124 --> 00:34:22,060
So how long has
this been going on?

492
00:34:22,227 --> 00:34:24,822
What are you talking
about? Nothing's going on.

493
00:34:25,196 --> 00:34:26,255
Nothing?

494
00:34:26,431 --> 00:34:28,271
Do all of your friends
have keys to your place?

495
00:34:29,067 --> 00:34:31,969
It's okay, Claire. She knows.

496
00:34:32,504 --> 00:34:35,474
- Were you here the night of murder?
- Yes.

497
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:37,404
Jimmy, that means
she can give you an alibi.

498
00:34:37,576 --> 00:34:41,069
- You've gotta come forward with this.
- See, she can't give me an alibi.

499
00:34:41,246 --> 00:34:42,612
I was here, but Claire wasn't.

500
00:34:43,281 --> 00:34:46,410
I parked in the fire lane
behind the library that night,

501
00:34:46,585 --> 00:34:48,213
and my car was towed.

502
00:34:48,386 --> 00:34:50,048
I got here about 11:00.

503
00:34:50,221 --> 00:34:51,689
And that's why I lied to Ben.

504
00:34:52,157 --> 00:34:56,117
Claire's just recently been divorced,
and I was seeing her before that.

505
00:34:56,294 --> 00:34:58,661
And the church is
very important to Claire,

506
00:34:58,830 --> 00:35:01,629
so I decided not to make
it more difficult for her.

507
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:04,480
And because telling the truth wouldn't
have done me any good anyway.

508
00:35:04,636 --> 00:35:06,867
<i>In college basketball,
scandal-rocked Baxter</i>

509
00:35:07,038 --> 00:35:09,337
<i>- takes on Iowa Tech at The Barn.</i>
- Excuse me.

510
00:35:09,507 --> 00:35:11,840
<i>Despite the loss of
outside gunner Ron Sipes</i>

511
00:35:12,010 --> 00:35:13,478
<i>and volatile coach Jimmy Nash,</i>

512
00:35:13,645 --> 00:35:16,581
<i>the Cougars are still ten-point
favorites. In other hoop action...</i>

513
00:35:17,015 --> 00:35:19,416
- Cougars are still gonna win, honey.
- Hold on.

514
00:35:19,584 --> 00:35:24,454
<i>And out west, surging Stanford
is picked to beat USC by four.</i>

515
00:35:27,459 --> 00:35:30,258
Call Ben, tell him to
meet me at my office.

516
00:35:34,265 --> 00:35:35,756
Those numbers are point spreads.

517
00:35:36,167 --> 00:35:38,534
I went through all 26 games
that we played last season

518
00:35:38,703 --> 00:35:41,639
and I wrote down the
point spread for each game,

519
00:35:41,806 --> 00:35:43,570
which is apparently
what Red did.

520
00:35:43,742 --> 00:35:45,074
Why are some of them circled?

521
00:35:45,243 --> 00:35:48,008
Because those are the games
where we didn't cover the point spread.

522
00:35:48,179 --> 00:35:50,080
We either lost by more
than we were expected to

523
00:35:50,248 --> 00:35:52,683
or we won by less than
we were expected to.

524
00:35:52,851 --> 00:35:54,114
And if Red looked at this tape,

525
00:35:54,285 --> 00:35:56,914
he would have discovered
what I discovered.

526
00:36:00,358 --> 00:36:01,986
In every single
one of these games

527
00:36:02,160 --> 00:36:04,322
it was Barry Glover who
made or missed the shot

528
00:36:04,496 --> 00:36:06,021
that determined the final score.

529
00:36:06,364 --> 00:36:07,923
Barry was throwing the games?

530
00:36:08,099 --> 00:36:09,431
He was shaving points.

531
00:36:09,601 --> 00:36:12,537
Probably making
a fortune in bets.

532
00:36:13,571 --> 00:36:18,066
Red loved the Cougars more
than life, and he bought players,

533
00:36:18,243 --> 00:36:19,973
but he never would
have stood for that.

534
00:36:20,245 --> 00:36:21,713
Daggone.

535
00:36:24,616 --> 00:36:27,552
I said "daggone." I
didn't say "damn."

536
00:36:41,866 --> 00:36:43,801
You know, Red told his wife

537
00:36:43,968 --> 00:36:46,008
that he was going out to
do some errands that night.

538
00:36:46,171 --> 00:36:47,298
Hmm.

539
00:36:47,472 --> 00:36:49,634
- Wonder where he went.
- I don't know.

540
00:36:49,808 --> 00:36:51,608
What about his effects?
Find anything unusual?

541
00:36:51,743 --> 00:36:53,336
- No.
- Hmm.

542
00:36:53,511 --> 00:36:56,242
Hmm, hmm, hmm.

543
00:36:56,414 --> 00:36:59,441
- Here's that crazy car of his.
- Oh.

544
00:36:59,617 --> 00:37:01,279
The Cougarmobile.

545
00:37:01,453 --> 00:37:03,053
- He drove that thing everywhere.
- Yeah.

546
00:37:03,221 --> 00:37:05,747
Yeah. I used to see him
around town blowing his horn

547
00:37:05,924 --> 00:37:08,359
and waving like he was some
kind of homecoming queen.

548
00:37:12,430 --> 00:37:15,025
I didn't see it
outside, did you?

549
00:37:15,867 --> 00:37:17,768
- Uh-uh.
- Huh.

550
00:37:17,936 --> 00:37:20,371
Wonder how he
got here that night.

551
00:37:22,173 --> 00:37:25,109
Well, now, that's
a good question.

552
00:37:25,276 --> 00:37:29,008
If he had driven here that night,
he'd have parked his car right here.

553
00:37:29,180 --> 00:37:31,843
Old Red, God rest
his soul, kind of lazy.

554
00:37:32,016 --> 00:37:34,096
Believed in parking as
close to the door as he could.

555
00:37:34,252 --> 00:37:35,379
Yeah.

556
00:37:35,787 --> 00:37:37,119
Whose car is that?

557
00:37:37,288 --> 00:37:39,985
Well, I didn't know.

558
00:37:40,625 --> 00:37:44,118
It's not from our dealership, so
I called the automobile registry

559
00:37:44,295 --> 00:37:49,757
and found out it's registered to
somebody named Daniel Downing.

560
00:37:49,934 --> 00:37:52,631
It was purchased
the very day Red died.

561
00:37:53,404 --> 00:37:55,100
Keys are right here.

562
00:37:55,707 --> 00:37:59,109
It's brand-new, all right. It's
got less than 100 miles on it.

563
00:38:01,246 --> 00:38:03,442
Different dealership.

564
00:38:04,549 --> 00:38:08,145
I bet Red left his
car over there,

565
00:38:08,319 --> 00:38:10,311
drove this car here so
he could deliver it to...

566
00:38:10,488 --> 00:38:11,968
- What was his name?
- Daniel Downing.

567
00:38:12,123 --> 00:38:15,116
Yeah. Later that night.

568
00:38:18,029 --> 00:38:19,998
What are you doing?

569
00:38:20,165 --> 00:38:21,793
Thinking.

570
00:38:22,467 --> 00:38:24,436
Pop this thing here.

571
00:38:32,644 --> 00:38:34,306
It's empty.

572
00:38:45,056 --> 00:38:46,957
What happened there?

573
00:38:47,125 --> 00:38:49,492
Uh, I missed.

574
00:38:49,661 --> 00:38:51,721
The Cougars were
favored to win by six.

575
00:38:51,896 --> 00:38:55,890
You missed two foul
shots. They won by four.

576
00:38:56,201 --> 00:38:57,396
What about this game?

577
00:39:02,607 --> 00:39:04,576
I screwed up again.

578
00:39:04,742 --> 00:39:07,041
And because of it, the
Cougars won by three

579
00:39:07,212 --> 00:39:09,579
instead of the five
they were supposed to.

580
00:39:09,747 --> 00:39:11,807
- What about this game?
- Your Honor.

581
00:39:11,983 --> 00:39:16,114
Mr. Matlock, I take it
this, uh, blooper reel

582
00:39:16,287 --> 00:39:18,654
is somehow relevant
to the case at hand?

583
00:39:18,823 --> 00:39:21,850
- Yes, sir.
- Then tell us how now.

584
00:39:22,026 --> 00:39:23,050
Yes, sir.

585
00:39:24,529 --> 00:39:26,498
Tell me something, Barry.

586
00:39:26,664 --> 00:39:31,102
Why is it that all last season,
most of your screw-ups

587
00:39:31,269 --> 00:39:33,534
came at the last
minutes of the game?

588
00:39:34,639 --> 00:39:37,108
Even great players get
tight at the end of a game.

589
00:39:37,275 --> 00:39:39,676
But not quite as often as you.

590
00:39:39,844 --> 00:39:41,369
You were shaving
points, weren't you?

591
00:39:42,013 --> 00:39:43,037
No.

592
00:39:43,214 --> 00:39:46,184
You weren't being paid to
manipulate the point spread

593
00:39:46,351 --> 00:39:48,911
so that somebody could make
a killing betting on your games?

594
00:39:49,087 --> 00:39:50,487
No, that's a lie.

595
00:39:50,655 --> 00:39:52,556
You were doing it on your own?

596
00:39:53,691 --> 00:39:56,718
If I was shaving points for
myself or somebody else,

597
00:39:56,895 --> 00:39:58,659
either way, I'd be rich.

598
00:39:58,830 --> 00:40:01,299
And I'm not rich. I mean,
ask anyone who knows me.

599
00:40:01,466 --> 00:40:05,403
I live in a dorm. I drive a
beater. I had to borrow this coat.

600
00:40:05,570 --> 00:40:07,004
You're smart.

601
00:40:07,171 --> 00:40:08,799
You got a lot of
money, but you're smart.

602
00:40:08,973 --> 00:40:13,604
You figured this clever way to
hide your point-shaving money.

603
00:40:13,778 --> 00:40:17,374
- Your Honor.
- Mr. Matlock.

604
00:40:17,548 --> 00:40:19,744
I'm about to bring it
home, Your Honor.

605
00:40:20,418 --> 00:40:21,909
Bring it on in.

606
00:40:23,788 --> 00:40:27,555
When's the last time you spoke
to the decedent, Red Tolman?

607
00:40:28,960 --> 00:40:30,451
I don't know.

608
00:40:30,728 --> 00:40:33,288
Well, according to
the telephone records,

609
00:40:33,464 --> 00:40:36,992
he called your private
room in the dormitory

610
00:40:37,168 --> 00:40:41,196
on the 23rd of last month
at 3:09 in the afternoon.

611
00:40:41,372 --> 00:40:43,238
That sound about right?

612
00:40:43,608 --> 00:40:44,906
Yeah, I guess.

613
00:40:45,076 --> 00:40:47,739
Hmm. That was the
day he was murdered.

614
00:40:47,912 --> 00:40:50,074
What did he wanna talk about?

615
00:40:50,815 --> 00:40:53,614
Basketball, I guess. That's all
he ever wanted to talk about.

616
00:40:53,785 --> 00:40:56,846
More specifically, he wanted to
talk about point shaving, didn't he?

617
00:40:57,021 --> 00:40:59,855
- No.
- Oh, come on, now.

618
00:41:00,124 --> 00:41:01,524
You see this piece of paper?

619
00:41:01,693 --> 00:41:03,719
It was found on Red's
desk in his house.

620
00:41:03,895 --> 00:41:04,919
See these numbers?

621
00:41:05,663 --> 00:41:07,689
See these circled numbers?

622
00:41:08,566 --> 00:41:12,003
He figured out that
you were shaving points

623
00:41:12,170 --> 00:41:15,436
and he was gonna
turn you in, wasn't he?

624
00:41:15,840 --> 00:41:17,035
- No.
- I bet he was.

625
00:41:17,208 --> 00:41:20,406
And I bet you begged
to talk to him first,

626
00:41:20,578 --> 00:41:23,980
- in private that night at his office.
- Never happened.

627
00:41:24,148 --> 00:41:27,346
Only you didn't meet him
there, you got there early.

628
00:41:27,518 --> 00:41:29,783
And you didn't talk to him.

629
00:41:29,954 --> 00:41:31,718
You hit him over the
head and killed him.

630
00:41:31,889 --> 00:41:34,586
- That's not true.
- And then you trashed the place

631
00:41:34,759 --> 00:41:36,751
and planted one of Coach
Nash's whistles there

632
00:41:36,928 --> 00:41:39,329
so it would look like he
had done it in a fit of anger.

633
00:41:39,497 --> 00:41:42,194
- I didn't do any of that.
- Yes, you did.

634
00:41:42,600 --> 00:41:44,694
And this proves it.

635
00:41:45,570 --> 00:41:47,129
See that?

636
00:41:48,539 --> 00:41:52,306
There's a basketball
player, Daniel Downing.

637
00:41:52,477 --> 00:41:55,447
Red was gonna pay him
to play for the Cougars.

638
00:41:55,613 --> 00:41:58,674
Thirty thousand dollars
in cash and a car,

639
00:41:58,850 --> 00:42:01,684
a brand-new white sports car.

640
00:42:01,853 --> 00:42:03,515
Coach Nash turned down the deal,

641
00:42:03,688 --> 00:42:06,055
but Red had the
money, he had the car.

642
00:42:06,224 --> 00:42:10,992
I saw it myself parked
in front of his dealership.

643
00:42:11,162 --> 00:42:14,189
The police searched
your room this morning.

644
00:42:14,365 --> 00:42:17,062
They found this briefcase.

645
00:42:17,435 --> 00:42:19,734
It's nice. I mean,
it's high quality.

646
00:42:21,105 --> 00:42:26,373
You can't buy a briefcase like
this but one place in this town.

647
00:42:26,577 --> 00:42:29,877
And Cliff Lewis had the shop
owner go through his records.

648
00:42:30,815 --> 00:42:32,807
And guess what.

649
00:42:33,684 --> 00:42:36,176
This briefcase
wasn't bought by you,

650
00:42:36,354 --> 00:42:39,153
it was bought by Red Tolman.

651
00:42:39,323 --> 00:42:40,552
Now, how could that be?

652
00:42:42,493 --> 00:42:44,359
How about this?

653
00:42:44,529 --> 00:42:47,021
After you killed him
and wrecked the place,

654
00:42:47,198 --> 00:42:49,064
you tried to leave.

655
00:42:49,233 --> 00:42:50,531
But you couldn't.

656
00:42:50,701 --> 00:42:54,468
Somebody had parked
a car behind your car.

657
00:42:55,440 --> 00:42:58,604
See, without thinking, you
had parked in Red's space,

658
00:42:58,776 --> 00:43:00,938
so he just pulled up behind you.

659
00:43:01,112 --> 00:43:04,640
So you went back inside, got
the key, came back outside,

660
00:43:04,816 --> 00:43:08,947
and moved the sports
car one space over.

661
00:43:09,353 --> 00:43:11,913
And you saw the briefcase.

662
00:43:12,090 --> 00:43:15,618
And you opened it and you saw
the $30,000, so you took them.

663
00:43:16,394 --> 00:43:20,331
And you squirreled that 30,000 away
with your other point-shaving money

664
00:43:20,998 --> 00:43:25,265
and hid the briefcase in
the closet of your dorm room,

665
00:43:25,670 --> 00:43:30,005
never imagining that one
day it would incriminate you.

666
00:43:30,274 --> 00:43:32,243
But it doesn't incriminate me.

667
00:43:32,410 --> 00:43:36,541
Red gave that briefcase to
me long before that, as a gift.

668
00:43:36,714 --> 00:43:38,114
No.

669
00:43:38,282 --> 00:43:40,513
The shop owner's records show

670
00:43:40,685 --> 00:43:44,281
that this briefcase was sold to
Red the day he was murdered.

671
00:43:44,455 --> 00:43:46,924
The only way you could
have wound up with it

672
00:43:47,091 --> 00:43:49,151
is if you killed him.

673
00:43:50,461 --> 00:43:53,158
You'd have been better off to
let him turn you in, wouldn't you?

674
00:43:58,970 --> 00:44:02,964
<i>Don't you hear The
bells now ringing?</i>

675
00:44:03,141 --> 00:44:06,976
<i>Don't you hear
the angels singing?</i>

676
00:44:07,678 --> 00:44:15,678
<i>'Tis the glory
hallelujah jubilee</i>

677
00:44:17,588 --> 00:44:22,720
<i>In that far-off sweet forever</i>

678
00:44:23,261 --> 00:44:28,962
<i>Just beyond the shining river</i>

679
00:44:29,133 --> 00:44:34,868
<i>When they ring the golden bells</i>

680
00:44:35,039 --> 00:44:43,039
<i>For you and me You and me</i>

681
00:44:44,415 --> 00:44:46,179
Yeah. Ha, ha.

682
00:44:46,350 --> 00:44:47,784
- Ah.
- That was great, huh?

683
00:44:47,952 --> 00:44:49,750
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- That was great, yeah.

684
00:44:49,921 --> 00:44:52,641
Let's go get some peanut butter
and crackers or popcorn or something.

685
00:44:52,790 --> 00:44:55,453
Yeah, yeah. All's well
that ends well. Yeah.

686
00:44:55,626 --> 00:44:58,425
- Jimmy's okay, the choir sounds good.
- Mm.

687
00:44:58,596 --> 00:45:00,929
All's well that ends well.

688
00:45:01,799 --> 00:45:04,428
- Yeah.
- Cliffy, I'm sorry about Claire.

689
00:45:04,602 --> 00:45:06,127
It wasn't meant to be.

690
00:45:06,304 --> 00:45:08,068
- She was pretty, though, wasn't she?
- Yeah.

691
00:45:10,041 --> 00:45:11,942
Is it ever gonna be meant to be?

692
00:45:12,109 --> 00:45:13,600
Well, sure.

693
00:45:13,778 --> 00:45:15,508
There's another old song:

694
00:45:15,680 --> 00:45:18,275
"I'm blue, but I
won't be blue always

695
00:45:18,449 --> 00:45:21,044
because the sun's gonna
shine in my back door someday."

696
00:45:22,420 --> 00:45:25,356
It'll be okay, Cliff. You
ain't over the hill yet.

697
00:45:25,523 --> 00:45:27,583
- You ain't even to it. Ha, ha.
- Yeah.

698
00:45:27,758 --> 00:45:29,693
Let's go toast with some
peanut butter and jelly.

699
00:45:29,860 --> 00:45:32,295
Oh, that sounds good.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
